\cf3 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The camera, probably a tablet camera, transports the viewer towards creaky ornate doors, the kind you you might imagine secluding a burlesque queen's boudoir. The doors sweep back and the view lurches unsteadily through a room where Time had once thrown party, then left without cleaning up. Shy light, as if embarrassed by the room's decline, came down in blurred puddles. Among the blurred motion images, tarnished chandeliers  resolve briefly into focus, with their cut-glass dulled by years of disregard. Patches of wallpaper, once as vibrant as a rose garden at high noon, peel and droop.\cf4 \
\cf5 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The camera pans; a once proud bar slouches along one wall, its mahogany top worn smooth; an expansive mirror, spotted and worn, reflects the room like an old vaudeville performer squinting through cataracts. Ghostly decanters stand guard, their spirits evaporated, sit on the dusty shelves.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heavy curtains, the colour of a cabaret singer's lipstick, hang faded and moth-eaten against the wall, leading the eye to a grand staircase. The staircase, its balustrade as intricately carved as a Victorian cameo but bruised by time, spirals up to a mezzanine of  secret rooms hidden by doors that sighed under the weight of their own stories.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'85almost smell the thickness of yesteryear's perfume, lingering in the room like a stubborn rumour that refused to be silenced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The camera pushes into the crowd occupying most of the ground floor; a crowd of leather, cowboy outfits, army uniforms, once glamorous evening dresses, and sequinned jumpsuits shimmering like disco balls caught in a cyclone. In the eye of the crowded storm, a figure in a suit studded with twinkling rhinestones, rises a little above the floor.\cf2 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91My fellow Elvi,\'92 the High Elvis says, his voice booming off the tired furnishings. \'91Praise the Dawkins.\'92 \
\cf6 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>the camera pushes forward over the lips of the room's belle of the ball, an object with  faded mosaics decorating its bottom; like the bleached tattoos of an ancient sailor, depicting mermaids seeming more world-weary than whimsical. The fixtures, once gleaming gold, had traded their lustre for the hue of an old pirate doubloon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I think it\'92s a spa bath,\'92\cf2 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis continued. \'91I am all shook up by the honour you bestow upon me. My hands are shaking and my knees are weak from the thought of such a fool as I being gifted this pocket full of rainbows. I have a burning love for all of you in the Ghetto. Suspicious minds may think I have taken this role to be the big boss man, but you are always on my mind and I love you so. As we travel along the long Jericho road, cross my heart and hope to die, we can make the morning and you\'92ll never walk alone. What we need is a little less conversation and a little more action so I washed my hands in muddy waters and they came out clean. It is no secret its a wonderful world and you\'92re the reason I\'92m living. If you give me all of your love, I\'92ll never stand in your way and there will be peace in the valley. I am pledging my love and let me be the one, but only the strong survive so please don\'92t stop loving me.\'92 He paused and the adoration of the Elvi spilled over into deafening applause and cheers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It is time we put aside our differences. It\'92s time we reclaimed our sense of pride, and our self-determination. It\'92s time for deeds and actions. We must begin with good works in our own home, the Ghetto, first.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Even though we rarely see the sun here under the Layer, in the shadows, under the great Airborne towers; we will create light in our hearts. Our spirits will rise from the shadows of the Ghetto and even from the Pale too, may their hopes and dreams shine brightly. We will make our home a place where families can visit without fear of robbery, rape or murder.\'92\
